# Review: LinearPlugin

The sync engine is real and well tested at the unit level. `operations/sync.ts` pulls a team's
projects and issues with a snapshot-driven three-way merge (remote wins on conflict), then pushes
only diverged fields through `projectUpdate` and `issueUpdate`, sending `null` to clear priority and
estimate. `sync.test.ts` covers the merge cases, title, status, priority and project-description
pushes, and set membership. `linear-api.ts` holds the GraphQL client with jittered exponential retry
on 429/5xx and the priority and status mappers.

The biggest divergence is the object model: Linear projects mirror as `TaskSet` objects (not
`Project`), and an issue's project is expressed as `TaskSet.tasks` membership, not a `Task.project`
ref. The Task status vocabulary is `started`, not `in-progress`, and estimates are converted from
points to t-shirt sizes. Workflow states are fetched whenever a team has any issues, not only when a
status push is needed. Untested: `GetLinearTeams`, `maxDaysBack`, soft-delete skipping, retry, and
most of the mapping table; no test runs T-1, T-7, T-8 or QA-1.

Of 48 items: 11 done, 20 untested, 8 partial, 5 diverged, 4 missing.

